00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.900
There are five common breeds of dairy cows.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:57.310
Ayrshire, Brown, Swiss, Z, Jersey, and Holstein-Friesian.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:02:59.960
The Holstein-Friesian...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.420
is the most popular because it can produce more milk than the other breeds.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:10.740
A cow is able to make milk when she is two years old and has given birth to a calf.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.140
Her milk is the food for her baby.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:19.950
She makes more than her calf will ever need, so we use the extra milk.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:29.640
In the spring and summer, a dairy cow eats grass and drinks water from streams and ponds.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:35.940
In the cold months, she is fed hay, winter silage, and grains.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.820
The better her food is, the more milk she will make, and the better her milk will be.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:52.300
The food eaten by a dairy cow is tough and coarse, and it is hard to digest.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:59.380
Some of the digested food goes into the cow's bloodstream, and finally enters her udder.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:18.220
the milk is made. The udder has four nipples or teats. Milk will come out of her teats. A cow can

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:25.970
be milked by hand. To do this, the farmer grasps a teat in each of his hands and squeezes it with

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:29.980
thumb and forefinger. Then he gently pulls...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:41.390
his hand down the teeth the milk squirts out into a pail today most farmers use milking machines

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:47.740
which are quicker and cleaner and pump sucks the milk through rubber lined cups that fit

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:55.060
over the cow's teeth the action of the cups is like the sucking of a calf the milk then

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:58.700
moves through a pipeline to a cooling tank in a separate room

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.460
Now, the milk is ready to take a trip.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.800
Every day, a big, shiny truck comes to the farm.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:14.440
Milk is pumped into the tank of the truck, which is insulated to keep it cool.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.160
The tank truck carries the milk to the dairy.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:24.660
When the truck arrives at the dairy, the milk is pumped into a big, refrigerated storage tank.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:28.880
The milk is moved into a clarifier to be cleaned.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:36.620
Then, the milk moves on to being pasteurized, which means it is heated to kill any disease

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:37.700
causing bacteria.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:44.740
Next, the milk is homogenized or forced through tiny openings under great pressure.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:50.260
This breaks up the fatty globules to give every drop of milk the same amount.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:54.240
Finally, the milk is packaged.

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:58.730
The milk moves through pipes to automatic packaging machines.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:08.680
These machines fill and seal the milk into paper cartons or plastic jugs.

00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:13.680
Dates are printed on them to show how long the milk will stay fresh.

